# Exercise: Queue Depth
#
# Little's Law: L = λW
# L = average number of items in the system (queue depth).
#
# When items arrive faster than they're processed, L grows.
# Here we send 3 items instantly but each takes 20ms to process.
# With 1 worker, items queue up!
#
# Your task: Receive all 3 results from `results` and sum them.
# (Each item starts at 1 and the worker adds 1 → expected sum is
# `(1+1) × 3 = 6`.)
#
# Stack convention: the spawned worker captures `work` and
# `results` via closure. After `strand.spawn drop`, main keeps
# `( work results )` on the stack. We send three 1s into `work`
# using `over` so the channels stay accessible, then receive
# three results from `results` the same way.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-queue-depth ( -- )
    chan.make   # work
    chan.make   # results

    # Spawn 1 slow worker: receive, sleep 20ms, add 1, send — three times.
    # Worker captures ( work results ) by closure; `over` copies the
    # work channel each iteration so it survives the receive.
    [
        over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
        over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
        over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
        drop drop
    ] strand.spawn drop
    # Stack: ( work results )

    # Send 3 items into the work channel (each is 1).
    over 1 swap chan.send drop
    over 1 swap chan.send drop
    over 1 swap chan.send drop

    # Your code here:
    # 1. Receive 3 results from the results channel and sum them.
    # 2. Use `nip nip` to drop work + results, leaving just the sum.
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    6 test.assert-eq
;
